* [[Floriana Lima]] as [[Maggie Sawyer|Margarita "Maggie" Sawyer]] (season 2; recurring: season 3): A detective for the National City Police Department who takes a special interest in the cases involving aliens and metahumans.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stanhope |first=Kate |date=July 13, 2016 |title='Supergirl' Adds 'The Family' Grad as Gay DC Character Maggie Sawyer (Exclusive) |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/supergirl-season-2-maggie-sawyer-910498 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160714150041/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/supergirl-season-2-maggie-sawyer-910498 |archive-date=July 14, 2016 |access-date=July 13, 2016 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> The first openly [[gay]] character introduced, Maggie dates Alex Danvers, even becoming engaged. However, this is broken off. Lima became a recurring actress for the third season, departing in the season's fifth episode. Lima noted the role was only intended to last for one season.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Swift |first=Andy |date=May 26, 2017 |title=Supergirl: Floriana Lima Steps Down as Series Regular, Will Recur in Season 3 |url=https://www.tvline.com/casting-news/supergirl-floriana-lima-season-3-status-maggie-recurring-834708/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170526221001/http://tvline.com/2017/05/26/supergirl-floriana-lima-season-3-status-maggie-recurring/ |archive-date=May 26, 2017 |access-date=May 27, 2017 |website=TVLine}}</ref>
* [[Katie McGrath]] as [[Lena Luthor|Lena Kieran Luthor]] (seasons 3–6; recurring: season 2):<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=March 22, 2017 |title='Supergirl': Katie McGrath Promoted To Series Regular For Season 3 |url=https://deadline.com/2017/03/supergirl-katie-mcgrath-promoted-series-regular-season-3-lena-luthor-the-cw-1202048294/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170601015933/http://deadline.com/2017/03/supergirl-katie-mcgrath-promoted-series-regular-season-3-lena-luthor-the-cw-1202048294/ |archive-date=June 1, 2017 |access-date=June 1, 2017 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref> The CEO of L-Corp (formerly known as Luthor Corp) and the younger paternal half-sister of [[Lex Luthor]]. Camille Marty portrays a young Lena.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Supergirl Radio Season 2 – Episode 12: Luthors |url=https://www.acast.com/supergirlradio/supergirl-radio-season-2---episode-12-luthors |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170303002834/https://www.acast.com/supergirlradio/supergirl-radio-season-2---episode-12-luthors |archive-date=March 3, 2017 |access-date=March 2, 2017 |website=Acast}}</ref>
* [[Odette Annable]] as [[Reign (DC Comics)|Samantha "Sam" Arias / Reign]] (season 3; guest: season 5): Another Kryptonian sent to Earth as an infant and single mother to her daughter Ruby. Samantha's villainous alternate personality, Reign, emerges in the middle of season 3, but she is unaware of it and her alter ego's actions.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=May 31, 2017 |title=Supergirl: Odette Annable cast as season 3 big bad Reign |url=https://ew.com/tv/2017/05/31/supergirl-odette-annable-cast-as-season-3-big-bad-reign/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170601004121/http://ew.com/tv/2017/05/31/supergirl-odette-annable-cast-as-season-3-big-bad-reign/ |archive-date=June 1, 2017 |access-date=May 31, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=October 9, 2017 |title=Supergirl Boss Previews a 'Lot More' Sister Scenes, Reign's Rise, Lena's Foe and One 'Raw, Amazing' Episode |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/supergirl-season-3-preview-875193/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171010011649/http://tvline.com/2017/10/09/supergirl-season-3-preview/ |archive-date=October 10, 2017 |access-date=October 10, 2017 |website=TVLine}}</ref>
* [[Jesse Rath]] as [[Brainiac 5|Querl "Brainy" Dox / Brainiac 5]] (seasons 4–6; recurring: season 3): <br />A half-[[Artificial intelligence|A.I.]], half-organic 12th-level intellect from the planet Colu and a member of the [[Legion of Super-Heroes]] in the 31st century.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=June 15, 2018 |title='Supergirl': Jesse Rath Upped To Series Regular For Season 4 |url=https://deadline.com/2018/06/supergirl-jesse-rath-promoted-series-regular-season-4-the-cw-1202411077/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180616002233/https://deadline.com/2018/06/supergirl-jesse-rath-promoted-series-regular-season-4-the-cw-1202411077/ |archive-date=June 16, 2018 |access-date=June 19, 2018 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>
* [[Sam Witwer]] as [[Agent Liberty|Benjamin Lockwood / Agent Liberty]] (season 4; guest: season 5):  The brilliant, ruthless, and terrifying founder and figurehead of Children of Liberty, a human-supremacist hate group that supports a human-first world order.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=July 17, 2018 |title=Smallville alum Sam Witwer joins Supergirl season 4 as Agent Liberty |url=https://ew.com/tv/2018/07/17/smallville-sam-witwer-supergirl-season-4-agent-liberty/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180717235703/http://ew.com/tv/2018/07/17/smallville-sam-witwer-supergirl-season-4-agent-liberty/ |archive-date=July 17, 2018 |access-date=July 17, 2018}}</ref>
* [[Nicole Maines]] as [[Nia Nal|Nia Nal / Dreamer]] (seasons 4–6): <br />A soulful young [[Trans woman|transgender woman]] with a fierce drive to protect others and the newest addition to the CatCo reporting team. The character is the first [[transgender]] superhero on television.<ref name="MainesJones">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=July 21, 2018 |title='Supergirl' Adds Nicole Maines As Transgender Superhero; April Parker Jones & David Ajala Also Cast – Comic-Con |url=https://deadline.com/2018/07/supergirl-nicole-maines-transgender-superhero-april-parker-jones-david-ajala-1202430870/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180722010450/https://deadline.com/2018/07/supergirl-nicole-maines-transgender-superhero-april-parker-jones-david-ajala-1202430870/ |archive-date=July 22, 2018 |access-date=July 21, 2018 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>
* [[April Parker Jones]] as Colonel Lauren Haley (season 4):  A hardline career military woman who lives and dies by the orders of her commanding officers. Dedicated to her country, she always acts in its best interest — even if it's not her own.<ref name="MainesJones" />
* [[Azie Tesfai]] as Kelly Olsen (seasons 5–6; recurring: season 4): James's younger, no-nonsense sister, recently returned to the United States following a military tour overseas.
* [[Andrea Brooks]] as [[Eve Teschmacher]] (season 5; recurring: seasons 2–4; guest: season 6): A former CatCo assistant who became part of Lena's research team at L-Corp before being revealed as a dastardly turncoat spy working for [[Lex Luthor]]. 
* [[Julie Gonzalo]] as [[Acrata|Andrea Rojas / Acrata]] (seasons 5–6): A CEO of Obsidian Tech who is the new editor-in-chief of Catco Worldwide Media and an old friend of Lena Luthor.
* [[Staz Nair]] as William Dey (seasons 5–6): A new star reporter at Catco Worldwide Media who is secretly remains under the London Times newspapers' employ and undercover to investigates Andrea Rojas, suspecting that she is a criminal.
* [[LaMonica Garrett]] as [[Monitor (Mar Novu)|Mar Novu / Monitor]] (season 5; guest: season 4): A multiversal being testing different Earths in the multiverse in preparation for an impending "[[Crisis on Infinite Earths (Arrowverse)|crisis]]", providing the Book of Destiny to John Deegan, releasing J'onn J'onzz's [[Malefic J'onzz|brother]], and retrieving the corpse of [[Lex Luthor (Arrowverse)|Lex Luthor]]. He made his first appearance in the Arrowverse crossover "[[Elseworlds (Arrowverse)|Elseworlds]]".
** Garrett also portrays [[Anti-Monitor (Arrowverse)|Mobius / Anti-Monitor]], the Monitor's polar opposite, an evil being dedicated to ending the multiverse.<ref name="BatwomanPressRelease">{{Cite web |title=(#108) "Crisis on Infinite Earths: Part Two" |url=http://www.thefutoncritic.com/listings/20191121cw03/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20191122035054/http://www.thefutoncritic.com/listings/20191121cw03/ |archive-date=November 22, 2019 |access-date=November 21, 2019 |website=[[The Futon Critic]]}}</ref><ref name="Crisissynopsis">{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=November 21, 2019 |title='Crisis on Infinite Earths' Crossover Spoilers: The Search for 'Smallville,' Sara vs. Mia, Black Lightning and More |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/crisis-on-infinite-earths-spoilers-flash-supergirl-batwoman-episodes-1127491/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191121222951/https://tvline.com/2019/11/21/crisis-on-infinite-earths-spoilers-flash-supergirl-batwoman-episodes/ |archive-date=November 21, 2019 |access-date=November 21, 2019 |website=TVLine}}</ref>
* [[Peta Sergeant]] as [[Nyxlygsptlnz|Nyxlygsptlnz "Nyxly"]] (season 6): A 5th Dimension Imp princess who Kara meets in the Phantom Zone, who desires revenge on her father for banishing her and killing her brother, and is willing to do whatever is necessary to get it.<ref name="Sergeant">{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=April 9, 2021 |title='Supergirl': Peta Sergeant Joins Season 6 As New Series Regular |url=https://deadline.com/2021/04/supergirl-peta-sergeant-nyxly-final-season-cw-series-1234730718/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210409160151/https://deadline.com/2021/04/supergirl-peta-sergeant-nyxly-final-season-cw-series-1234730718/ |archive-date=April 9, 2021 |access-date=April 9, 2021 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>

==Production==
===Development===
In September 2014, [[Warner Bros. Television Studios|Warner Bros. Television]] initiated development on a [[Supergirl]] television series, with [[Greg Berlanti]], [[Ali Adler]], and [[Sarah Schechter (producer)|Sarah Schechter]] serving as executive producers under [[Berlanti Productions]]. [[DC Comics]]' [[Geoff Johns]] was also attached to the project. Early working titles included ''Super'' and ''Girl''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=September 4, 2014 |title=Supergirl Takes Flight With TV Series From Greg Berlanti & Ali Adler |url=https://deadline.com/2014/09/supergirl-tv-series-dc-comics-829364/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140904233413/http://deadline.com/2014/09/supergirl-tv-series-dc-comics-829364/ |archive-date=September 4, 2014 |access-date=September 5, 2014 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>

Berlanti cited inspiration from actress [[Ginger Rogers]] for the character's portrayal, a concept that resonated with executive producer Schechter.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sunu |first=Steven |date=September 9, 2014 |title=Berlanti Confirms "Supergirl" TV Series Reports |url=http://www.comicbookresources.com/?page=article&id=55398 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140911001814/http://www.comicbookresources.com/?page=article&id=55398 |archive-date=September 11, 2014 |access-date=September 9, 2014 |website=Comic Book Resources}}</ref><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=December 2, 2019 |title=From Arrow to 'Crisis': Inside the evolution of the Arrowverse and its crossovers |url=https://ew.com/tv/2019/12/02/arrowverse-crisis-on-infinite-earths-crossovers-history/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191202171200/https://ew.com/tv/2019/12/02/arrowverse-crisis-on-infinite-earths-crossovers-history/ |archive-date=December 2, 2019 |access-date=December 2, 2019 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]}}</ref>

On September 20, 2014, [[CBS]] committed to the series, planning a premiere for the [[2015–16 United States network television schedule|2015–16 television schedule]]. In January 2015, CBS Entertainment chairwoman [[Nina Tassler]] described the series as a [[procedural drama]] blending serialized arcs with crime-solving elements.<ref name="EWJan2015">{{Cite magazine |last=Hibberd |first=James |date=January 12, 2015 |title=CBS reveals 'Supergirl' details: The costume is 'awesome' |url=http://insidetv.ew.com/2015/01/12/supergirl/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150117110739/http://insidetv.ew.com/2015/01/12/supergirl/ |archive-date=January 17, 2015 |access-date=January 22, 2015 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref>[[File:Jessica Queller and Robert Rovner at SDCC 2019 roundtable.jpg|thumb|Jessica Queller and [[Robert Rovner]] at the ''Supergirl'' Roundtable at the 2019 [[San Diego Comic-Con]]]]<nowiki> </nowiki>[[Melissa Benoist]] was cast as Supergirl in January 2015 after an extensive audition process, with other actresses considered including [[Claire Holt]] and [[Gemma Atkinson]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kenneally |first=Tim |date=December 1, 2014 |title='Vampire Diaries' Star Claire Holt in Contention for CBS's 'Supergirl' Series (Exclusive) |url=https://www.thewrap.com/vampire-diaries-star-claire-holt-in-contention-for-cbss-supergirl-series-exclusive/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141202035438/http://www.thewrap.com/vampire-diaries-star-claire-holt-in-contention-for-cbss-supergirl-series-exclusive/ |archive-date=December 2, 2014 |access-date=December 1, 2014 |website=The Wrap}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Roffman |first=Marisa |date=December 8, 2014 |title='The Originals' Boss on the Fallout From the Rebekah Twist |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/originals-boss-fallout-rebekah-twist-754765 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150102015444/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/originals-boss-fallout-rebekah-twist-754765 |archive-date=January 2, 2015 |access-date=December 8, 2014 |website=The Hollywood Reporter}}</ref>

[[Blake Neely]], known for his work on ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' and ''[[The Flash (2014 TV series)|The Flash]]'', was confirmed as the series' composer in March 2015.<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=579163922797711360 |user=cowonthewall |title=@darthtardis: @MoreTVMag @cowonthewall @PSPatel Will the awesome Blake Neely be scoring the new spinoff or Supergirl?; @cowonthewall: @darthtardis @MoreTVMag @PSPatel yes to both! Woohoo!! |first=Blake |last=Neely |author-link=Blake Neely |date=March 21, 2015 |access-date=March 22, 2015}}</ref>

The show was officially picked up to series on May 6, 2015, and the [[Television pilot|pilot]] premiered at [[San Diego Comic-Con]] in July 2015.<ref name="Officialpick1">{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=May 6, 2015 |title='Supergirl' Ordered to Series at CBS |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/supergirl-ordered-series-at-cbs-793201 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150508233208/http://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/supergirl-ordered-series-at-cbs-793201 |archive-date=May 8, 2015 |access-date=May 7, 2015 |website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref><ref name="Airdate1">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=May 13, 2015 |title=CBS Fall 2015 Schedule: 'Supergirl' Opens Monday, 'Life In Pieces' Follows 'Big Bang' |url=https://deadline.com/2015/05/cbs-fall-schedule-2015-upfronts-new-shows-supergirl-life-in-pieces-1201425659/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151212162733/http://deadline.com/2015/05/cbs-fall-schedule-2015-upfronts-new-shows-supergirl-life-in-pieces-1201425659/ |archive-date=December 12, 2015 |access-date=May 13, 2015 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Fitzpatrick |first=Kevin |date=June 9, 2015 |title=CBS 'Supergirl' Sets October Premiere, Plus More Fall Schedules |url=https://screencrush.com/cbs-supergirl-premiere-october-26/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150610090043/http://screencrush.com/cbs-supergirl-premiere-october-26/ |archive-date=June 10, 2015 |access-date=June 9, 2015 |website=Screencrush}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldman |first=Eric |date=June 10, 2015 |title=Comic-Con: WBTV Bringing 18 Shows, Including Arrow, The Flash and Supergirl |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/06/10/comic-con-wbtv-bringing-18-shows-including-arrow-the-flash-and-supergirl |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150612075116/http://www.ign.com/articles/2015/06/10/comic-con-wbtv-bringing-18-shows-including-arrow-the-flash-and-supergirl |archive-date=June 12, 2015 |access-date=June 11, 2015 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref>

Adler noted that [[Superman]]'s role would be minimal to maintain focus on Supergirl. In November 2015, CBS ordered seven more episodes, bringing the total for [[Supergirl season 1|Season 1]] to 20.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Natalie |date=July 8, 2015 |title='Supergirl': How often will we see Superman? |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2015/07/06/supergirl-superman-cbs |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150710184012/http://www.ew.com/article/2015/07/06/supergirl-superman-cbs |archive-date=July 10, 2015 |access-date=July 11, 2015 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wagmeister |first=Elizabeth |date=November 30, 2015 |title='Supergirl' Lands Full First Season Pickup at CBS |url=https://variety.com/2015/tv/news/supergirl-season-1-pickup-full-order-cbs-melissa-benoist-1201642068/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151201154527/http://variety.com/2015/tv/news/supergirl-season-1-pickup-full-order-cbs-melissa-benoist-1201642068/ |archive-date=December 1, 2015 |access-date=November 30, 2015 |website=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |publisher=[[Penske Media Corporation]]}}</ref>

On May 12, 2016, it was announced that the series would move to [[The CW]] for [[Supergirl season 2|Season 2]], premiering in October 2016.<ref name="Season2Move">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=May 12, 2016 |title='Supergirl' Moves To The CW, Renewed For Season 2 |url=https://deadline.com/2016/05/supergirl-moves-the-cw-renewed-season-2-melissa-benoist-1201754579/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160513052542/http://deadline.com/2016/05/supergirl-moves-the-cw-renewed-season-2-melissa-benoist-1201754579/ |archive-date=May 13, 2016 |access-date=May 12, 2016 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Harewood |first=David |date=May 12, 2016 |title=22 episode order. Vancouver. CW. Details to come.#season2 #off2bed #Johannesburg |url=https://twitter.com/DavidHarewood/status/730874024524451840 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180618083247/https://twitter.com/DavidHarewood/status/730874024524451840 |archive-date=June 18, 2018 |access-date=May 12, 2016 |publisher=[[Twitter]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ge |first=Linda |date=May 19, 2016 |title='Supergirl' Could Lose Calista Flockhart as Series Regular in Season 2 |url=https://www.thewrap.com/supergirl-could-lose-calista-flockhart-as-series-regular-in-season-2/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160521181539/http://www.thewrap.com/supergirl-could-lose-calista-flockhart-as-series-regular-in-season-2/ |archive-date=May 21, 2016 |access-date=May 19, 2016 |website=The Wrap}}</ref>

Concerns were raised about [[Calista Flockhart]]'s availability, as her contract required filming in [[Los Angeles]]; she eventually returned in a recurring capacity.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Hibberd |first=James |date=May 19, 2016 |title=The CW boss finally says it: Supergirl should have been ours |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2016/05/19/supergirl |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160522001105/http://www.ew.com/article/2016/05/19/supergirl |archive-date=May 22, 2016 |access-date=May 19, 2016 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=August 1, 2016 |title='Supergirl': Calista Flockhart To Recur In Season 2 On CW As Filming Moves North |url=https://deadline.com/2016/08/supergirl-calista-flockhart-recur-in-season-2-the-cw-filming-canada-1201796814/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160802180404/http://deadline.com/2016/08/supergirl-calista-flockhart-recur-in-season-2-the-cw-filming-canada-1201796814/ |archive-date=August 2, 2016 |access-date=August 1, 2016 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>

The series was renewed for [[Supergirl season 3|Season 3]] in January 2017, with Jessica Queller and [[Robert Rovner]] taking over as co-showrunners.<ref name="S3Renewal">{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt |date=January 8, 2017 |title=The CW Renews ''The Flash'', ''Supernatural'', ''Crazy Ex'' and 4 Others |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-flash-renewed-season-4-supernatural-season-13-782389/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170109113246/http://tvline.com/2017/01/08/the-flash-renewed-season-4-supernatural-season-13/ |archive-date=January 9, 2017 |access-date=January 8, 2017 |website=TVLine}}</ref><ref name="CWFallPremiereDates">{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=June 8, 2017 |title=The CW Sets Fall 2017 Premiere Dates For 'Dynasty' & 'Valor' And Returning Series Returning Series |url=https://deadline.com/2017/06/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-dynasty-valor-the-flash-1202109659/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170611132346/http://deadline.com/2017/06/the-cw-fall-premiere-dates-dynasty-valor-the-flash-1202109659/ |archive-date=June 11, 2017 |access-date=June 8, 2017 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=June 16, 2017 |title='Supergirl': Jessica Queller & Robert Rovner Named Co-Showrunners On the CW Series |url=https://deadline.com/2017/06/supergirl-jessica-queller-robert-rovner-coshowrunners-cw-series-1202115025/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170619000110/http://deadline.com/2017/06/supergirl-jessica-queller-robert-rovner-coshowrunners-cw-series-1202115025/ |archive-date=June 19, 2017 |access-date=June 17, 2017 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

''Supergirl'' was renewed for a [[Supergirl season 4|fourth season]], which premiered October 14, 2018, followed by a [[Supergirl season 5|fifth season]] in October 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=April 2, 2018 |title='Riverdale,' 'Flash,' 'Supernatural' Among 10 CW Renewals |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/riverdale-flash-supernatural-10-cw-renewals-1098997 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180402195748/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/riverdale-flash-supernatural-10-cw-renewals-1098997 |archive-date=April 2, 2018 |access-date=April 2, 2018 |website=The Hollywood Reporter}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Swift |first=Andy |date=June 20, 2018 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates, Including New 'Super' Sunday |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/cw-premiere-dates-fall-2018-flash-charmed-riverdale-954928/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180620225706/https://tvline.com/2018/06/20/cw-premiere-dates-fall-2018-flash-charmed-riverdale/ |archive-date=June 20, 2018 |access-date=June 20, 2018 |work=TVLine |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=January 31, 2019 |title=CW Renews 'The Flash,' 'Charmed,' 'Riverdale,' 'Supernatural,' 6 More |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-renews-flash-charmed-riverdale-supernatural-6-more-1181714 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190202015411/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/cw-renews-flash-charmed-riverdale-supernatural-6-more-1181714 |archive-date=February 2, 2019 |access-date=January 31, 2019 |website=The Hollywood Reporter}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Petski |first=Denise |date=June 17, 2019 |title=The CW Sets Fall Premiere Dates: 'Batwoman', 'Supergirl', 'The Flash', 'Nancy Drew', More |url=https://deadline.com/2019/06/the-cw-sets-fall-premiere-dates-batwoman-supergirl-the-flash-nancy-drew-1202633821/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190618053243/https://deadline.com/2019/06/the-cw-sets-fall-premiere-dates-batwoman-supergirl-the-flash-nancy-drew-1202633821/ |archive-date=June 18, 2019 |access-date=June 17, 2019 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref>

On January 7, 2020, the series was renewed for a [[Supergirl season 6|sixth season]], which premiered on March 30, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Porter |first=Rick |date=January 7, 2020 |title=The CW Renews 13 Series for 2020-21, on Track for No Cancellations |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/batwoman-riverdale-flash-10-more-series-renewed-at-cw-1267596 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200108003942/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/batwoman-riverdale-flash-10-more-series-renewed-at-cw-1267596 |archive-date=January 8, 2020 |access-date=January 7, 2020 |website=The Hollywood Reporter |language=en}}</ref><ref name="2021SeasonStartDates">{{Cite web |last=White |first=Peter |date=March 5, 2021 |title='Superman & Lois' Temporally Moves Off The CW Schedule Due To Previous Covid-19 Pause, 'Supergirl' Flies Into Help |url=https://deadline.com/2021/03/superman-lois-the-cw-schedule-supergirl-1234707899/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210415080758/https://deadline.com/2021/03/superman-lois-the-cw-schedule-supergirl-1234707899/ |archive-date=April 15, 2021 |access-date=March 5, 2021 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

On September 22, 2020, it was announced the series would conclude after season six, ending in November 2021.<ref name="Final">{{Cite web |last=Swift |first=Andy |date=September 22, 2020 |title=Supergirl Ending With Season 6 |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/supergirl-ending-season-6-final-episodes-the-cw-1234578081/ |access-date=September 22, 2020 |website=TVLine}}</ref>

===Design===
[[File:BenoistSupergirl.jpg|thumb|upright|[[Supergirl (Kara Zor-El)|Supergirl]]'s design was intended to be a modern take on the classic look of the character.]]
The original Supergirl costume was designed by [[Colleen Atwood]], who previously worked on ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' and ''[[The Flash (2014 TV series)|The Flash]]''. Atwood aimed to "embrace the past" while modernizing the look.<ref name="EWJan2015" />

[[Melissa Benoist]] noted the costume’s short skirt as daunting but accepted it as part of the challenge.<ref name="CBRint" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Epsiten |first=Ian |date=October 22, 2015 |title=How TV's Supergirl Got Her New Look |url=https://www.vulture.com/2015/10/colleen-atwood-supergirl-costume.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151024150350/http://www.vulture.com/2015/10/colleen-atwood-supergirl-costume.html |archive-date=October 24, 2015 |access-date=October 24, 2015 |website=vulture.com}}</ref>

The design featured a high-neck top, pleated skirt, tights, and boots, with practical elements like a cape fastened to an undersuit and Eurojersey fabric.<ref name="MMCBR" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lesnick |first=Silas |date=March 6, 2015 |title=It's a Bird! It's a Plane! It's the first Supergirl images! |url=http://www.superherohype.com/news/332643-its-a-bird-its-a-plane-its-the-first-supergirl-images#/slide/1 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150308153053/http://www.superherohype.com/news/332643-its-a-bird-its-a-plane-its-the-first-supergirl-images#/slide/1 |archive-date=March 8, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |website=SuperHeroHype}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Wagmeister |first=Elizabeth |date=March 6, 2015 |title='Supergirl' Costume Revealed: First Photos of Melissa Benoist as CBS Superhero |url=https://variety.com/2015/tv/news/supergirl-costume-revealed-photos-melissa-benoist-cbs-1201447987/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150306222744/http://variety.com/2015/tv/news/supergirl-costume-revealed-photos-melissa-benoist-cbs-1201447987/ |archive-date=March 6, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |website=Variety}}</ref>

Reception was mixed. Natalie Abrams of ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'' praised the modern update and the more covered look, while ''[[Screen Rant]]''’s Andrew Dyce applauded the nostalgic elements.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Natalie |date=March 6, 2015 |title=Supergirl debuts new costume – what's different? |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2015/03/06/supergirl-debuts-new-costume-whats-different |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150308055252/http://www.ew.com/article/2015/03/06/supergirl-debuts-new-costume-whats-different |archive-date=March 8, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |magazine=Entertainment Weekly}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Dyce |first=Andrew |date=March 6, 2015 |title='Supergirl's Full Costume Revealed in First Official Images |url=https://screenrant.com/supergirl-tv-show-suit-images/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150308121442/http://screenrant.com/supergirl-tv-show-suit-images/ |archive-date=March 8, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |website=ScreenRant}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Cavna |first=Michael |date=March 6, 2015 |title=First look: Melissa Benoist's Supergirl costume revealed by CBS |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/comic-riffs/wp/2015/03/06/first-look-melissa-benoists-supergirl-costume-revealed-by-cbs/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150310144147/http://www.washingtonpost.com/news/comic-riffs/wp/2015/03/06/first-look-melissa-benoists-supergirl-costume-revealed-by-cbs/ |archive-date=March 10, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref>

Conversely, ''[[E!|E! Online]]'' and ''[[TV Guide]]'' criticized the outfit for its colors and resemblance to a Halloween costume.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 6, 2015 |title=All The Greatest Superhero Costumes On TV—Ranked From Super Tragic To Super Epic!: Supergirl on ''Supergirl'' |url=http://www.eonline.com/photos/14534/all-the-greatest-superhero-costumes-on-tv-ranked-from-super-tragic-to-super-epic/470337 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150307021132/http://www.eonline.com/photos/14534/all-the-greatest-superhero-costumes-on-tv-ranked-from-super-tragic-to-super-epic/470337 |archive-date=March 7, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |website=E! Online}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gennis |first=Sadie |date=March 6, 2015 |title=Get a First Look at CBS' Supergirl Costume |url=https://www.tvguide.com/news/cbs-supergirl-costume/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150308050651/http://www.tvguide.com/news/cbs-supergirl-costume/ |archive-date=March 8, 2015 |access-date=March 6, 2015 |website=TV Guide}}</ref>

In the [[Supergirl season 5|fifth season]], Supergirl’s costume was redesigned to feature pants, a practical departure from the skirt.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 20, 2019 |title='Supergirl': The CW Unveils Kara's New Skirtless Supergirl Suit |url=https://www.thewrap.com/new-supergirl-suit-first-look-cw-kara-melissa-benoist-photo/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191012225607/https://www.thewrap.com/new-supergirl-suit-first-look-cw-kara-melissa-benoist-photo/amp/ |archive-date=October 12, 2019 |access-date=October 13, 2019 |website=The Wrap}}</ref>

===Filming===
[[Andrew Kreisberg]] joined the series as writer and executive producer in February 2015. [[Glen Winter]] was named pilot director.<ref name="LeighHarewoodKreisberg" /><ref>{{Cite tweet |number=571159140975964161 |user=Glen__Winter |title=Incredibly proud and humbled to be directing the #supergirl pilot! Grateful to @GBerlanti @geoffjohns @AJKreisberg @AliAdler |first=Glen |last=Winter |author-link=Glen Winter |date=February 26, 2015}}</ref>

[[Principal photography]] for the pilot occurred from March 4–29, 2015, on the [[Warner Bros.]] lot in [[Burbank, California]]—previously used for ''[[Lois & Clark: The New Adventures of Superman]]''.<ref name="Filming1">{{Cite web |last=Melrose |first=Kevin |date=March 4, 2015 |title="Supergirl" Pilot Starts Production |url=https://www.cbr.com/supergirl-pilot-starts-production/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150305052238/http://www.comicbookresources.com/article/supergirl-pilot-stars-production |archive-date=March 5, 2015 |access-date=March 4, 2015 |website=Comic Book Resources}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jayson |first=Jay |date=March 29, 2015 |title=CBS' Supergirl Has Wrapped Filming |url=https://comicbook.com/2015/03/29/cbs-supergirl-has-wrapped-filming/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150331210837/http://comicbook.com/2015/03/29/cbs-supergirl-has-wrapped-filming/ |archive-date=March 31, 2015 |access-date=March 30, 2015 |website=Comic Book.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Phegley |first=Kiel |date=March 16, 2015 |title=CBS' "Supergirl" Includes Old School DC TV Nod On Set |url=https://www.cbr.com/cbs-supergirl-includes-old-school-dc-tv-nod-on-set/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160318231306/http://www.comicbookresources.com/article/cbs-supergirl-includes-old-school-dc-tv-nod-on-set |archive-date=March 18, 2016 |access-date=March 19, 2015 |website=Comic Book Resource}}</ref>

The show had an estimated production cost of $3 million per episode, among the highest for a freshman series.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Collins |first=Scott |date=May 4, 2016 |title='Supergirl' Renewal Drama: Talks Center on Possible Jump to CW, Budget Cuts (Exclusive) |url=https://www.thewrap.com/supergirl-renewal-drama-talks-center-on-possible-jump-to-cw-budget-cuts-exclusive/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160505035810/http://www.thewrap.com/supergirl-renewal-drama-talks-center-on-possible-jump-to-cw-budget-cuts-exclusive/ |archive-date=May 5, 2016 |access-date=May 5, 2016 |website=[[TheWrap]]}}</ref><ref name="Season2Move" />

In [[Supergirl season 2|Season 2]], production moved to [[Vancouver]] to cut costs, with filming beginning July 25, 2016, and ending April 26, 2017.<ref name="Season2Move" />{{Quote_box
| width = 45%
| align = Centre|"When I was a child, my planet, Krypton, was dying. I was sent to Earth to protect my cousin. But my pod got knocked off-course, and by the time I got here, my cousin had already grown up to become... Superman. And so, I hid my powers, until recently when an accident forced me to reveal myself to the world. To most people, I am an assistant at CatCo Worldwide Media. But in secret, I work with my adoptive sister for the D.E.O. to protect my city from alien life and anyone else that means to cause harm. I am Supergirl!"|- Opening monologue from the first season.
}}Vancouver remained the filming base through the final season, with scenes shot throughout the city to depict National City.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Takeuchi |first=Craig |date=April 23, 2018 |title=Filming in Vancouver: Arrow, Supergirl, and Fast Layne wrap but The Order gets underway |url=https://www.insidevancouver.ca/2018/04/23/filming-in-vancouver-arrow-supergirl-and-fast-layne-wrap-but-the-order-gets-underway/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180917181833/https://www.insidevancouver.ca/2018/04/23/filming-in-vancouver-arrow-supergirl-and-fast-layne-wrap-but-the-order-gets-underway/ |archive-date=September 17, 2018 |access-date=September 17, 2018 |website=Inside Vancouver}}</ref>

On March 12, 2020, production was suspended due to the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Goldberg |first=Lesley |date=March 12, 2020 |title=Coronavirus Impact: Netflix Shuts Down Film, TV Work in U.S. and Canada as Production Nears Standstill |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/tv-productions-flux-coronavirus-concerns-1284398 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200315044450/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/live-feed/tv-productions-flux-coronavirus-concerns-1284398 |archive-date=March 15, 2020 |access-date=March 15, 2020 |website=The Hollywood Reporter}}</ref>

Filming for the sixth season was delayed due to issues receiving [[COVID-19 testing|COVID-19 test]] results, ultimately beginning later in fall 2020.<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 4, 2020 |title=DGC BC Production List |url=https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200904184853/https://www.dgc.ca/assets/Uploads/BritishColumbia/AvailsProductionLists/Documents/Production-List.pdf |archive-date=September 4, 2020 |access-date=September 4, 2020 |work=[[Directors Guild of Canada]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Aurthur |first=Kate |date=September 30, 2020 |title='Riverdale,' 'Batwoman' Shut Down Over Testing Delays in British Columbia |url=https://variety.com/2020/tv/news/riverdale-batwoman-shut-down-coronavirus-testing-1234787386/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930080341/https://variety.com/2020/tv/news/riverdale-batwoman-shut-down-coronavirus-testing-1234787386/ |archive-date=September 30, 2020 |access-date=September 30, 2020 |work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=September 29, 2020 |title=U.S. TV Production in Vancouver Largely On Pause Over COVID-19 Test Results Delays |url=https://deadline.com/2020/09/production-in-vancouver-on-pause-ovid-19-test-results-delays-1234588472/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200930151855/https://deadline.com/2020/09/production-in-vancouver-on-pause-ovid-19-test-results-delays-1234588472/ |archive-date=September 30, 2020 |access-date=September 30, 2020 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>

==Broadcast==
In Canada, ''Supergirl'' aired in a [[simultaneous substitution|sim-subbed]] [[simulcast]] on [[Global Television Network|Global]] alongside the American broadcast during the [[Supergirl season 1|first season]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Watch Supergirl TV Series Online |url=http://www.globaltv.com/supergirl/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20160307204357/http://www.globaltv.com/supergirl/ |archive-date=March 7, 2016 |access-date=September 11, 2015 |website=Global TV}}</ref> For the [[Supergirl season 2|second season]], the series moved to [[Showcase (Canadian TV channel)|Showcase]] under the same arrangement.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Furdyk |first=Brent |date=August 29, 2016 |title=Showcase Unveils Blockbuster Fall Lineup: 'Supergirl', 'Travelers', 'Incorporated' And More |url=http://etcanada.com/news/162909/showcase-unveils-blockbuster-fall-lineup-supergirl-travelers-incorporated-and-more/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161122161350/http://etcanada.com/news/162909/showcase-unveils-blockbuster-fall-lineup-supergirl-travelers-incorporated-and-more/ |archive-date=November 22, 2016 |access-date=November 22, 2016 |work=[[ET Canada]]}}</ref>

In the United Kingdom, ''Supergirl'' premiered on [[Sky One]] on October 29, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Munn |first=Patrick |date=October 14, 2015 |title=Sky1 Sets UK Premiere Date For 'Supergirl' |url=http://www.tvwise.co.uk/2015/10/sky1-sets-uk-premiere-date-for-supergirl/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151015140126/http://www.tvwise.co.uk/2015/10/sky1-sets-uk-premiere-date-for-supergirl/ |archive-date=October 15, 2015 |access-date=October 19, 2015 |website=TVWise}}</ref>

In Australia, ''Supergirl'' premiered on [[Fox8|FOX8]] on December 6, 2015.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Knox |first=David |date=November 12, 2015 |title=Airdate: DC's Legends of Tomorrow |url=http://www.tvtonight.com.au/2015/11/airdate-dcs-legends-of-tomorrow.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151116141125/http://www.tvtonight.com.au/2015/11/airdate-dcs-legends-of-tomorrow.html |archive-date=November 16, 2015 |access-date=November 12, 2015 |website=TV Tonight}}</ref>

