==Arrowverse==
{{See also|Arrowverse}}
In November 2014, Berlanti expressed interest in ''Supergirl'' existing in the [[Arrowverse]], the same universe as his other series ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' and ''[[The Flash (2014 TV series)|The Flash]]'',<ref>{{Cite magazine |last1=Stack |first1=Tim |last2=Brissey |first2=Breia |date=December 5, 2014 |title=The Man Behind the Masks |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |publisher=[[Time Warner]] |issue=1340 |pages=42–46}}</ref><ref>{{Cite AV media |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I1GOM-kNjq8 |title=Vixen: Series Premiere Featurette: CW Seed |date=August 21, 2015 |last=CW Seed |publisher=YouTube |time=0:11 |access-date=August 22, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151207185609/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=I1GOM-kNjq8 |archive-date=December 7, 2015 |url-status=live}}</ref> and in January 2015, [[The CW]] president Mark Pedowitz revealed that he was also open to a crossover between the series and networks (due to Berlanti executive producing all three and The CW being co-owned by CBS). However, CBS Entertainment chair Nina Tassler stated that month that "those two shows are on a different network. So I think we'll keep ''Supergirl'' to ourselves for a while."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cornet |first=Roth |date=January 12, 2015 |title=CBS President Nina Tassler Doubtful About a Supergirl/Flash/Arrow Crossover |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/01/12/cbs-president-nina-tassler-doubtful-about-a-supergirlflasharrow-crossover |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150115035229/http://www.ign.com/articles/2015/01/12/cbs-president-nina-tassler-doubtful-about-a-supergirlflasharrow-crossover |archive-date=January 15, 2015 |access-date=January 22, 2015 |website=IGN}}</ref> In August 2015, Tassler revealed that while there were no plans at the time to do crossover storylines, the three series would have crossover promotions.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Abram |first=Natalie |date=August 10, 2015 |title=CBS edges closer to 'Supergirl,' 'Arrow,' 'The Flash' crossover |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2015/08/10/cbs-edges-closer-supergirl-arrow-flash-crossover |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150812221850/http://www.ew.com/article/2015/08/10/cbs-edges-closer-supergirl-arrow-flash-crossover |archive-date=August 12, 2015 |access-date=August 11, 2015}}</ref>

Pedowitz regretted passing on the series when presented it in mid-2014, saying, "We hadn't launched ''The Flash'' yet, we weren't ready to take on another DC property. In hindsight we probably should've gone that direction...Sometimes you lose great shows."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=August 11, 2015 |title=CW Boss Has 'Supergirl' Regret, Talks Possible Crossover, 'Jane the Virgin' Emmy Snub & 'Supernatural' End Date |url=https://deadline.com/2015/08/supergirl-regret-crossover-jane-the-virgin-emmy-snub-supernatural-end-spinoff-1201497547/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150914164422/http://deadline.com/2015/08/supergirl-regret-crossover-jane-the-virgin-emmy-snub-supernatural-end-spinoff-1201497547/ |archive-date=September 14, 2015 |access-date=September 18, 2015 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref> In January 2016, during the [[Television Critics Association]] press tour, he said that The CW was still interested in a crossover with ''Supergirl'' if the producers could find a way to do it,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wagmeister |first=Elizabeth |date=January 10, 2016 |title=CW President Teases Female Superhero Show, Talks 'Supernatural' Future and Stands By 'Crazy Ex-Girlfriend' |url=https://variety.com/2016/tv/news/the-cw-female-superhero-show-vixen-1201676210/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160111164937/http://variety.com/2016/tv/news/the-cw-female-superhero-show-vixen-1201676210/ |archive-date=January 11, 2016 |access-date=January 10, 2016 |website=Variety}}</ref> and Berlanti added that while no official conversations had taken place, internal ones had concerning how a crossover would work. He also noted that for a crossover to happen during ''Supergirl''{{'s}} first season, it would have to be figured out "in the next month or so".<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Hibberd |first=James |date=January 10, 2016 |title=Supergirl evolving as Flash crossover deadline nears |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2016/01/10/supergirl-crossover |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160111150428/http://www.ew.com/article/2016/01/10/supergirl-crossover |archive-date=January 11, 2016 |access-date=January 10, 2016}}</ref> Glenn Geller, Tassler's successor at CBS, then stated on the matter, "I have to be really careful what I say here. Watch and wait and see what happens."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ge |first=Linda |date=January 12, 2016 |title=CBS President Teases 'Supergirl'-'The Flash' Crossover |url=https://www.thewrap.com/cbs-president-teases-supergirl-the-flash-crossover/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160114045637/http://www.thewrap.com/cbs-president-teases-supergirl-the-flash-crossover/ |archive-date=January 14, 2016 |access-date=January 12, 2016 |website=TheWrap}}</ref>

On February 3, 2016, it was announced that [[Grant Gustin]], who appears as [[Barry Allen (Arrowverse)|Barry Allen / Flash]] on ''The Flash'', would appear in the eighteenth episode of the first season, "[[Worlds Finest]]".<ref name="Ep18Title">{{Cite web |title=(#SG118) "Worlds Finest" |url=http://thefutoncritic.com/listings/20160309cbs01/ |access-date=March 9, 2016 |website=The Futon Critic}}</ref> While no plot details on the episodes were released at the time, Ross A. Lincoln of ''[[Deadline Hollywood]]'' noted that "the in-universe reason" for the crossover was due to Barry's ability to travel to various dimensions, thus implying that ''Supergirl'' exists on an alternate Earth to ''Arrow'' and ''The Flash'' in a multiverse.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lincoln |first=Ross A. |date=February 3, 2016 |title=Crossover Confirmed: 'The Flash' To Meet 'Supergirl' In March Episode |url=https://deadline.com/2016/02/supergirl-the-flash-crossover-official-in-march-1201695796/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160204034257/http://deadline.com/2016/02/supergirl-the-flash-crossover-official-in-march-1201695796/ |archive-date=February 4, 2016 |access-date=February 3, 2016 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref> ''The Flash'' episode "[[Welcome to Earth-2]]" confirmed this, showing an image of Benoist as Supergirl during a sequence where characters travel through that multiverse.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Couto |first=Anthony |date=February 9, 2016 |title=Surprising DC Superhero Makes Cameo On "The Flash" |url=https://www.cbr.com/surprising-dc-superhero-makes-cameo-on-the-flash/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160212195031/http://www.comicbookresources.com/article/surprising-dc-superhero-makes-cameo-flash |archive-date=February 12, 2016 |access-date=February 9, 2016 |website=[[Comic Book Resources]]}}</ref> The earth that the series inhabits is Earth-38 in the Arrowverse multiverse,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Day |first=Carla |date=November 29, 2016 |title='The Flash' Recap: "Invasion!" – The Gang's All Here |url=https://collider.com/the-flash-recap-invasion/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161130190328/http://collider.com/the-flash-recap-invasion/ |archive-date=November 30, 2016 |access-date=November 30, 2016 |website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]}}</ref> and has been informally referred to as "Earth-CBS" by [[Marc Guggenheim]], one of the creators of ''Arrow''.<ref name=GuggenheimOct2016>{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Natalie |date=October 18, 2016 |title=Arrow boss teases 100th episode, crossover catalyst |url=https://www.ew.com/article/2016/10/18/arrow-crossover-100-episode-spoilers |url-status=live |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161021095428/http://www.ew.com/article/2016/10/18/arrow-crossover-100-episode-spoilers |archive-date=October 21, 2016 |access-date=October 18, 2016}}</ref>

During the second season, Supergirl appears in "[[Invasion! (Arrowverse)|Invasion!]]", a crossover episode of ''The Flash'', ''Arrow'' and ''[[Legends of Tomorrow]]'',<ref name=GuggenheimOct2016/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Burlingame |first=Russ |date=October 23, 2016 |title=Marc Guggenheim Reveals the Title Of Arrow's 100th Episode |url=https://comicbook.com/2016/10/23/marc-guggenheim-reveals-the-title-of-arrows-100th-episode/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220062349/http://comicbook.com/dc/2016/10/23/marc-guggenheim-reveals-the-title-of-arrows-100th-episode/ |archive-date=December 20, 2016 |access-date=October 23, 2016 |website=ComicBook.com}}</ref> when she's recruited by Barry Allen and Cisco Ramon at the end of "[[Medusa (Supergirl)|Medusa]]" to help fight off an invasion by the [[Dominators (DC Comics)|Dominators]].<ref name=GuggenheimOct2016/> ''Supergirl'' and ''The Flash'' also featured in a musical crossover, featuring several covers of existing songs along with two original numbers.<ref name="MonElMusicalCrossover" /> Similar to "Invasion!", the crossover begins at the end of the ''Supergirl'' episode "[[Star-Crossed (Supergirl)|Star-Crossed]]" and primarily takes place during ''The Flash'' episode "[[Duet (The Flash)|Duet]]", featuring the [[Music Meister]] as the antagonist who puts both The Flash and Supergirl in a shared hallucination.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schwartz |first=Terri |date=January 20, 2017 |title=The Flash / Supergirl Crossover Episode Details Revealed |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2017/01/20/the-flash-supergirl-crossover-episode-details-revealed |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170120195452/http://www.ign.com/articles/2017/01/20/the-flash-supergirl-crossover-episode-details-revealed |archive-date=January 20, 2017 |access-date=January 20, 2017 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref> After "Invasion!", Guggenheim felt "If there's an appetite for it from the fans and from the network," the crossover next year could be "a proper four-part crossover."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bryant |first=Jacob |date=November 29, 2016 |title=Marc Guggenheim Teases Mega-Crossover, Celebrating 'Arrow's' 100th Episode |url=https://variety.com/2016/tv/news/cw-crossover-arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-crossover-marc-guggenheim-1201928834/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201120418/http://variety.com/2016/tv/news/cw-crossover-arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-crossover-marc-guggenheim-1201928834/ |archive-date=December 1, 2016 |access-date=December 6, 2016 |website=Variety}}</ref>

At the 2017 [[Paleyfest]] event, Kreisberg reiterated the creative team's intention to do a full four-way crossover the following year.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bryant |first=Jacob |date=March 19, 2017 |title=Kevin Smith Helps Celebrate the CW's 'Arrowverse' at PaleyFest |url=https://variety.com/2017/tv/news/arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-kevin-smith-paleyfest-1202011595/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180119095907/http://variety.com/2017/tv/news/arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-of-tomorrow-kevin-smith-paleyfest-1202011595/ |archive-date=January 19, 2018 |access-date=January 28, 2018}}</ref> At [[San Diego Comic-Con|San Diego Comic Con]] in 2017, it was confirmed that another four-way crossover would take place, with Supergirl playing a larger role than the previous season. The four-way crossover event, titled "[[Crisis on Earth-X]]", took place on November 27 and 28, 2017, across ''Supergirl'' and ''Arrow'' (on the first night) and ''The Flash'' and ''Legends of Tomorrow'' (on the second night).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=August 2, 2017 |title=Arrow-verse's Next 4-Show Crossover Event Set for Two Nights in November |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-crossover-november-2017-853680/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170813104955/http://tvline.com/2017/08/02/arrow-flash-supergirl-legends-crossover-november-2017/ |archive-date=August 13, 2017 |access-date=August 13, 2017 |website=TVLine}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Bryant |first=Jacob |date=August 2, 2017 |title=CW Announces Dates for 'Arrowverse' Crossover Episodes |work=Variety |url=https://variety.com/2017/tv/news/arrowverse-crossover-episodes-dates-cw-supergirl-flash-1202513591/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 13, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170813110649/http://variety.com/2017/tv/news/arrowverse-crossover-episodes-dates-cw-supergirl-flash-1202513591/ |archive-date=August 13, 2017}}</ref>

In May 2018, ''[[Arrow (TV series)|Arrow]]'' star [[Stephen Amell]] announced at The CW [[Upfront (advertising)|upfronts]] that the next [[Arrowverse]] crossover would feature [[Batwoman]] and [[Gotham City]]. The crossover, titled "[[Elseworlds (Arrowverse)|Elseworlds]]", aired in December 2018, ahead of a potential 2019 solo series for the character.<ref name="Batwoman">{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Natalie |date=May 17, 2018 |title=Next CW superhero crossover to introduce Batwoman |url=https://ew.com/tv/2018/05/17/cw-superhero-crossover-batwoman/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180706075132/http://ew.com/tv/2018/05/17/cw-superhero-crossover-batwoman/ |archive-date=July 6, 2018 |access-date=July 8, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Swift |first=Andy |date=July 17, 2018 |title=Batwoman TV Series in Development, Will Feature Out Lesbian Kate Kane |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/batwoman-tv-series-the-cw-lesbian-kate-kane-arrowverse-964651/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180717175909/https://tvline.com/2018/07/17/batwoman-tv-series-the-cw-lesbian-kate-kane-arrowverse/ |archive-date=July 17, 2018 |access-date=July 17, 2018 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> ''Supergirl'' was confirmed to have a participating episode in August, which closed out the three-part crossover, trading nights with ''The Flash'' just for the event. Therefore, the show's participating episode aired on Tuesday, December 11.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nolan |first=Emma |date=August 24, 2018 |title=Arrowverse crossover 2018 air date, cast, trailer plot: When will it air? |url=https://www.express.co.uk/showbiz/tv-radio/1008126/Arrowverse-crossover-2018-air-date-cast-trailer-plot-The-Flash-Supergirl-Arrow-Superman-CW/amp |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180906195732/https://www.express.co.uk/showbiz/tv-radio/1008126/Arrowverse-crossover-2018-air-date-cast-trailer-plot-The-Flash-Supergirl-Arrow-Superman-CW/amp |archive-date=September 6, 2018 |access-date=September 6, 2018 |website=Express}}</ref> The end of "Elseworlds" teased the next crossover event, "[[Crisis on Infinite Earths (Arrowverse)|Crisis on Infinite Earths]]".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=December 11, 2018 |title=Arrowverse Crossover Title for Fall 2019 (!) Is Revealed — And It Is a Doozy |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/arrowverse-crossover-title-2019-crisis-on-infinite-earths-1010874/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181212024818/https://tvline.com/2018/12/11/arrowverse-crossover-title-2019-crisis-on-infinite-earths/ |archive-date=December 12, 2018 |access-date=December 14, 2019 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> ''Supergirl'''s episode opened the five-part crossover on December 8, 2019, with the final two installments airing on January 14, 2020.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=August 4, 2019 |title=Arrowverse 'Crisis on Infinite Earths' Crossover Adds Black Lightning Stars, Bruce Wayne — Get Full Schedule |url=https://www.tvline.com/casting-news/arrowverse-crisis-crossover-schedule-black-lightning-cast-1085926/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190804214914/https://tvline.com/2019/08/04/arrowverse-crisis-crossover-schedule-black-lightning-cast/ |archive-date=August 4, 2019 |access-date=December 14, 2019 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> At the end of the event, the new Earth-Prime was formed, which saw Earth-38 merged with the former Earth-1 and ''[[Black Lightning]]''{{'}}s earth, creating a fictional universe where all of the CW series exist together.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Agard |first=Chancellor |date=January 14, 2020 |title='Crisis on Infinite Earths' introduces a major change for the CW's superhero shows |url=https://ew.com/tv/2020/01/14/crisis-on-infinite-earths-introduces-major-change-cws-superhero-shows/ |url-status=live |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200116031914/https://ew.com/tv/2020/01/14/crisis-on-infinite-earths-introduces-major-change-cws-superhero-shows/ |archive-date=January 16, 2020 |access-date=January 15, 2020}}</ref>

===Standalone spin-off===
{{Main|Superman & Lois}}

In October 2019, The CW and Warner Bros. Television announced development on a spin-off series titled ''Superman & Lois'', with [[Tyler Hoechlin]] and [[Elizabeth Tulloch]] reprising their roles as Clark Kent / Superman and Lois Lane.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mitovich |first=Matt Webb |date=October 28, 2019 |title=''Superman & Lois'' Series in Development at The CW, Starring Tyler Hoechlin and Elizabeth Tulloch — Get Details |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/superman-lois-lane-series-in-development-the-cw-1118178/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191028204247/https://tvline.com/2019/10/28/superman-lois-lane-series-in-development-the-cw/ |archive-date=October 28, 2019 |access-date=October 28, 2019 |website=TVLine}}</ref> In January 2020, ''Superman & Lois'' was ordered to series.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andreeva |first=Nellie |date=January 14, 2020 |title=''Superman & Lois'' And ''Walker, Texas Ranger'' Reboot With Jared Padalecki Get CW Series Orders |url=https://deadline.com/2020/01/superman-lois-walker-texas-ranger-reboot-with-jared-padalecki-get-cw-series-orders-1202830309/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200114205444/https://deadline.com/2020/01/superman-lois-walker-texas-ranger-reboot-with-jared-padalecki-get-cw-series-orders-1202830309/ |archive-date=January 14, 2020 |access-date=January 14, 2020 |website=Deadline Hollywood}}</ref> The series premiered on February 23, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ausiello |first=Michael |date=May 14, 2020 |title=The CW Delays New Season Until 2021: ''Superman & Lois'' to Follow ''Flash'', ''Walker'' Fills Winchester Void, ''Supergirl'' Delayed |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-fall-lineup-2020-schedule-delayed-superman-and-lois-1197597/ |access-date=May 14, 2020 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref><ref name="S&L premiere date">{{Cite web |last=Ausiello |first=Michael |date=October 29, 2020 |title=The CW Sets Delayed Premiere Dates for Flash, Superman & Lois, Riverdale, Charmed, Batwoman and More |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/the-cw-premiere-dates-2021-the-flash-superman-and-lois-1234589328/ |access-date=October 29, 2020 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref> Originally presented as being set in the same continuity as ''Supergirl'', the series was retroactively established to be set in an adjacent universe to the Arrowverse in its second season finale.<ref name="S&LNotArrowverse">{{Cite web |last=Swift |first=Andy |date=June 28, 2022 |title=How Is Superman & Lois Connected to the Arrowverse? EP Sheds More Light on Finale's Long-Awaited Explanation |url=https://www.tvline.com/news/superman-lois-arrowverse-connection-explained-finale-interview-1234846652/ |access-date=June 29, 2022 |website=[[TVLine]]}}</ref>

==Other media==

===Comic books===
In July 2015, a four-page preview comic entitled ''Sister Act'', written by [[Ali Adler]], [[Greg Berlanti]] and [[Andrew Kreisberg]] was released digitally online, and then a day later in the September 2015 issue of [[TV Guide]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Johnston |first=Eric |title=TV Guide Comic |url=https://supergirl.tv/tv-guide-comic |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180824205518/https://supergirl.tv/tv-guide-comic |archive-date=August 24, 2018 |access-date=August 24, 2018 |website=supergirl.tv}}</ref>

'''''Adventures of Supergirl''''': Beginning in January 2016, DC Comics launched a 13-issue bi-weekly digital comic (6 in print). Written by [[Sterling Gates]] and drawn by a rotating team of artist including Bengal, Jonboy Meyers, Emanuela Lupacchino, and Emma Vieceli, the comic, while not directly tying into the show, tells stories set in the universe of the show.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yehl |first=Joshua |date=December 15, 2015 |title=DC SWOOPS IN WITH ADVENTURES OF SUPERGIRL DIGITAL COMIC |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2015/12/15/dc-swoops-in-with-adventures-of-supergirl-digital-comic |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151218110007/http://www.ign.com/articles/2015/12/15/dc-swoops-in-with-adventures-of-supergirl-digital-comic |archive-date=December 18, 2015 |access-date=December 15, 2015 |website=IGN}}</ref> The digital series was collected in print as a six-issue series published twice a month from May to July 2016,<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Natalie |title=Supergirl TV series comes to life in comics |url=https://ew.com/article/2016/05/09/supergirl-comic-book/ |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180824183416/https://ew.com/article/2016/05/09/supergirl-comic-book/ |archive-date=August 24, 2018 |access-date=August 24, 2018}}</ref> and as a complete graphic novel in September of that year.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 16, 2016 |title=Adventures of Supergirl |url=https://www.dccomics.com/graphic-novels/adventures-of-supergirl |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180824182828/https://www.dccomics.com/graphic-novels/adventures-of-supergirl |archive-date=August 24, 2018 |access-date=August 24, 2018 |website=DC Comics}}</ref>

They are also involved in the ''Earth-Prime'' miniseries launched in April 2022.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arrant |first=Chris |date=February 18, 2022 |title=Stargirl and Legends of Tomorrow join DC's Arrowverse crossover event Earth-Prime |url=https://www.gamesradar.com/earth-prime-arrowverse-dc-cw/ |access-date=May 10, 2022 |website=gamesradar}}</ref>
* ''Adventures of Supergirl'' (2016-09-21<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.dc.com/graphic-novels/adventures-of-supergirl-2016/adventures-of-supergirl|title=ADVENTURES OF SUPERGIRL|website=DC}}</ref>): Includes ''Adventures of Supergirl'' #1-6.

===Novels===
In November 2017, [[Abrams Books]] began publishing a new trilogy of ''Supergirl'' novels, written by [[Jo Whittemore]], aimed at middle-grade readers in tandem with a similar trilogy of ''The Flash'' novels.<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Serrao |first=Nivea |title=''The Flash'' and ''Supergirl'' to get middle-grade novels |url=https://ew.com/books/2017/05/30/flash-supergirl-middle-grade-novels/ |url-status=live |magazine=Entertainment Weekly |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180824183315/https://ew.com/books/2017/05/30/flash-supergirl-middle-grade-novels/ |archive-date=August 24, 2018 |access-date=August 24, 2018}}</ref> The first, ''Supergirl: Age of Atlantis'', was released on November 7, 2017, and features Supergirl dealing with a surge of new powered people in National City, as well as a mysterious humanoid sea creature captured by the DEO who is seemingly attracted by the new superpowered people.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Whittemore |first=Jo |title=Supergirl: Age of Atlantis |date=November 7, 2017 |publisher=Harry N. Abrams |isbn=978-1419728143}}</ref> A sequel, ''Supergirl: Curse of the Ancients'', was released on May 1, 2018,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Whittemore |first=Jo |title=Supergirl: Curse of the Ancients |date=May 2018 |publisher=Harry N. Abrams |isbn=978-1419728662}}</ref> with a third novel, titled ''Supergirl: Master of Illusion'', released on January 8, 2019.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Whittemore |first=Jo |title=Supergirl: Master of Illusion |date=January 8, 2019 |publisher=Harry N. Abrams |isbn=978-1419731426}}</ref>

===Guidebook===
A guidebook for the series, published by Abrams, was released on March 12, 2019. ''Supergirl: The Secret Files of Kara Danvers: The Ultimate Guide to the Hit TV Show'' features "detailed profiles on characters and super powers, a heroes and villains gallery, episode guide, and more" from the first three seasons of the series.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Brothers |first=Warner |title=Amazon.com: Supergirl: The Secret Files of Kara Danvers: The Ultimate Guide to the Hit TV Show (9781419731709): Warner Brothers: Books |date=March 12, 2019 |publisher=Harry N. Abrams |isbn=978-1419731709}}</ref>

===Video games===
The video game ''[[Lego DC Super-Villains]]'' features DLC inspired by ''Supergirl'' in the "DC Super Heroes: TV Series DLC Character Pack". The DLC pack includes Supergirl as a playable character.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gutierrez |first=Gerardo |date=October 23, 2018 |title=LEGO DC Super Villains DC TV Series Super Heroes Character Pack |url=http://www.brickstolife.com/lego-dc-super-villains-super-heroes-tv-character-pack-dlc-early-access/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190321023001/http://www.brickstolife.com/lego-dc-super-villains-super-heroes-tv-character-pack-dlc-early-access/ |archive-date=March 21, 2019 |access-date=March 17, 2019 |website=Bricks To Life}}</ref>

